/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take two vectors A and B.
   - A signal on an interval a ≤ t ≤ b has one component for every instant.
   - a and b are the interval limits in seconds; y*(t) is the complex conjugate of y(t), and for real signals y*(t) = y(t).
   - ‖x‖ is the norm of x. ‖x‖² is the energy of x on [a, b] (Chapter 1.2), so the norm is the square root of the energy.
   - The inner product is one number.
   - Approximate a real signal f₁(t) by c f₂(t) over t₁ < t < t₂.
   - every integral runs from t₁ to t₂; c₁₂ is the component of f₁ along f₂, the same formula as c = A·B / |B|² for vectors.
   - With this c₁₂ the error energy is ‖f₁‖² − c₁₂²‖f₂‖²: the energy of f₁ minus the energy of the part that was kept.
   - If ⟨f₁, f₂⟩ = 0 then c₁₂ = 0.
   - A set of nonzero signals φ₁, φ₂, … is orthogonal on an interval when ⟨φₘ, φₙ⟩ = 0 for every m ≠ n.
*/
