/* book exam traps: Orthogonality depends on the interval. φ₁ = 1 and φ₂ = 2t − 1 have inner product 0 on [0, 1], but ∫₀² (2t − 1) dt = 4 − 2 = 2 on [0, 2]. | Divide by Kₙ = ‖φₙ‖², not by ‖φₙ‖. In worked example 2, dividing ⟨t, φ₂⟩ = 1/6 by ‖φ₂‖ = 0.577 gives 0.289 instead of the correct c₂ = 0.5. | For complex signals conjugate the second signal. Without the star, the inner product of ejt with itself over (0, 2π) comes out 0 instead of its energy 2π. | The best sine for the square wave is 1.273 sin t, not 1·sin t: it must be taller than the square wave to make up for being too low near t = 0 and t = π. */
