/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Approximate x(t) on [a, b] by the first N terms of an orthogonal set, Σ cₙφₙ(t) for n = 1 to N, with cₙ = ⟨x, φₙ⟩/Kₙ.
   - ε(N) is the error energy left after N terms; ∫|x(t)|² dt is the energy E of x on [a, b]; |cₙ|²Kₙ is the energy carried by the n-th term.
   - The set {φₙ} is complete on [a, b] when ε(N) → 0 as N → ∞ for every signal of finite energy on [a, b].
   - The equality holds in energy.
   - Completeness belongs to the set, not to one signal.
   - For a complete set the error energy reaches zero, so the energy of x equals the sum of the energies of its components.
   - the sum runs over every member of the set.
   - For a periodic signal, divide both sides by T₀ to get average power.
   - Check with Chapter 1.2: x(t) = 6 cos(200πt) + 3 has a₀ = 3 and a₁ = 6, so P = 3² + 6²/2 = 9 + 18 = 27 W, the value found there from the time signal.
   - Answer. cₙ = 4/(nπ) for odd n and 0 for even n; the terms n = 1 and 3 carry 90.06 % of the energy 2π; Σ 16/(n²π) over odd n = 2π = E.
*/
